@1-3
Only egui text types and std::path are needed: the result is a LayoutJob that TextEdit can render, plus a font, colours and the language enum.

@5-22
The supported languages. Plain is the fallback when detection fails or the file type is unknown.

@24-68
from_setting resolves the value stored in config.toml; from_path maps common file extensions to a language so auto detection needs no extra dependency. Some extensions are grouped, for example h with C and hpp with C++.

@70-110
Syntax rules per language: which line-comment prefixes to look for, whether /* */ or <!-- --> block comments exist, and which characters start strings. Shell, Python and JavaScript also accept backticks.

@112-197
Keyword tables per language. SQL is matched case-insensitively; JavaScript/TypeScript and shell allow $ in identifiers. Keeping the tables local avoids pulling in a full grammar crate.

@199-208
A Palette groups the seven token colours used by the highlighter. The plain text colour still comes from the active egui theme.

@210-269
Palette::new returns default, vivid or soft colours for dark or light mode. The lighter variants use darker accents so they stay readable on a light background.

@271-397
layout_job is a small line-oriented lexer. It walks the text, switches into block-comment mode across lines, handles Python triple-quoted strings, and classifies strings, numbers, identifiers, keywords, macros, function calls and type names. Every fragment is appended with the same monospace font so rows still align with the gutter.

@399-412
append skips empty fragments and copies the font into each TextFormat.

@414-431
scan_string finds the closing quote while respecting backslash escapes; if no closing quote is found on the line the caller decides whether the rest of the line is a string or a lone quote.

@433-443
scan_number consumes digits, letters, underscores and dots, which covers decimal, hexadecimal, binary and float literals without swallowing following operators.

@445-459
is_word_start and scan_word define identifier boundaries, including the optional leading $ for shell and JavaScript family languages.

@461-491
word_color gives keywords priority, then recognises Rust-style macro calls via a trailing !, function calls via a following (, and type-like names by their leading uppercase letter. Everything else uses the theme's plain text colour.
